LearnFromGames: text is looked for at its own size, the detector's pyramid is kept for the recognizer, and the
detector's threads keep off the slowest cores.

SetOCRTextHeight(pixels) tells the library how tall the smallest text on the pages to come is (LFG knows it from
its probe). The detector fits every page into 1280 x 1280 whatever is on it; given the height, it looks at the
scale that keeps that text 24 px tall when that is smaller, and a narrower page is then less work rather than
blown up again. Measured on a desktop with the probe's text height stood in for by the labelled lines':
994 Vita and PSP screens cut to the rows around their text, a read 22 ms -> 7 ms (median), characters wrong 7.5% ->
7.1%; cut to the text's columns too, 5 ms. What goes is mostly lines of nothing but dots or dashes (「…………」),
which grow too thin to find at a third of the size. Without a height (0, the default) the output is the same as
before, byte for byte.

The recognizer crops lines of large text from the detector's pyramid; it was built again for that (and the
detector's config read and parsed again). It is now handed over in PageLayout::pyramid.

The XNNPACK delegate starts its pool's threads when it is created, and they keep the cores they start on; the
detector's (four threads) now start on the faster cores, as the recognizer's do since 0004, and in fast mode the
calling thread keeps to them for the read. FasterCores moves from recognizer.cc to tflite_model.cc for that.

